Reads an OpenSSH client config (default ~/.ssh/config), resolves each
concrete Host to the HostName/Port/User that ssh would use, and emits a
valid SSHepherd manifest. users: and access: are derived from each host's
IdentityFile (or ssh's default identities when none is set) by reading the
matching .pub files on this machine — run import where you actually ssh
from, and review the result before use. Private keys are never read.
Keys named via --operator become operator users: granted every server
implicitly (no access entry) and protected from removal by future apply.
With --merge FILE the existing manifest FILE is preserved — comments
intact — and only what is genuinely new is appended; on any conflict the
manifest wins and the incoming entry is dropped with a warning.
Warnings about skipped entries go to stderr.

Exit codes:
  0  manifest emitted
  3  tool error: bad flags, unreadable ssh config or --merge manifest, an
     existing --output without --force, an in-place save refused

Usage:
  sshepherd import [ssh-config-path] [flags]

Examples:
  # Servers only, as the starting point for adopt
  sshepherd import --servers-only -o sshepherd.yaml

  # Servers and users from one config, with an operator key
  sshepherd import ~/.ssh/config --operator ~/.ssh/ops.pub -o sshepherd.yaml

  # Add hosts that are new in the ssh config to an existing manifest
  sshepherd import --merge sshepherd.yaml -o merged.yaml

Flags:
      --force                  allow --output to overwrite an existing file
  -h, --help                   help for import
      --merge FILE             merge into the existing manifest FILE: preserve its text (comments intact) and append only what is new
      --operator stringArray   key file whose owner is an operator: granted every server and protected from removal (repeatable)
  -o, --output string          write the manifest to this file instead of stdout
      --servers-only           leave the users and access sections empty (skip identity resolution)

Global Flags:
      --color string   colorize output: auto, always, or never (default "auto")
  -q, --quiet          log errors only
  -v, --verbose        enable debug logging
